(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 58-year-old man presents with several intensely itchy patches on his lower legs that appeared over two months. Examination shows multiple well-demarcated, coin-shaped erythematous plaques 1 to 5 cm in diameter scattered over both thighs and lower legs, with prominent scale and crusting. Each plaque is uniform in appearance throughout. What single examination finding most strongly separates this from the principal alternative diagnosis?","choices":["The plaques are uniform throughout, without central clearing","The plaques are well demarcated rather than poorly demarcated","The plaques are located on the extremities rather than the trunk","The plaques are intensely pruritic rather than painless","The plaques are round rather than irregular in outline"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The main differential is tinea corporis, and the slide draws the line exactly here: tinea corporis has central clearing and nummular eczema does not. Uniform appearance across the whole plaque is what makes this nummular eczema. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with diabetes has three thickened, discoloured toenails with crumbling of the distal nail plate, subungual debris, and separation of the plate from the bed. He also has scaling between the toes. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic paronychia","Onychogryphosis","Nail psoriasis","Nail unit melanoma","Onychomycosis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Distal lateral onychomycosis produces debris, onycholysis, thickening, discoloration, and crumbling, and dermatophytes — especially Trichophyton rubrum — cause most cases. Coexisting tinea pedis is a recognised risk factor. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with diabetes has an asymptomatic brownish patch in a skin fold noted on routine examination. Where is erythrasma most commonly found, and what is the typical symptom profile?","choices":["Axilla, groin, and perineum, recurrent and painful","Inner thighs, groin, and fourth toe web; often silent","Around the nose and mouth, honey-crusted","Face and lower leg, with sudden high fever","Scalp, thighs, and trunk, with follicular pustules"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The most common sites are the inner thighs, crural region, scrotum, and between the fourth and fifth toes, with the axilla, under the breasts, and intergluteal folds less common. It is usually asymptomatic and may be pruritic. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with recurrent genital herpes simplex virus type 2 infection is in a relationship with an uninfected partner. Which treatment approach also reduces transmission risk?","choices":["Patient-initiated episodic therapy","Daily suppressive valacyclovir","No antiviral therapy","Topical acyclovir during outbreaks","Prophylaxis for the uninfected partner"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent genital herpes may be managed with patient-initiated episodic therapy or daily suppressive therapy, and suppressive valacyclovir lowers herpes simplex virus type 2 transmission. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man in the American Southeast has a painful lesion on the thigh with a central violaceous area surrounded by a rim of blanched skin and then a large area of erythema. What is this sign, and what organism is responsible?","choices":["Crowe's sign, from a caterpillar reaction","The delta-wing jet sign, from a mite infestation","The red, white, and blue sign, from a black widow spider bite","The red, white, and blue sign, from a brown recluse spider bite","Hutchinson sign, from a hobo spider bite"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The hallmark of a brown recluse bite is the red, white, and blue sign — a central violaceous area surrounded by a rim of blanched skin surrounded by a large erythematous area. Loxosceles reclusa has a dark brown violin-shaped marking on the cephalothorax and is abundant in the American Midwest and Southeast. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has painful red lumps on both shins for ten days. Examination shows bilateral tender erythematous nodules 2 to 4 cm on the anterior tibial surfaces. None has ulcerated, and several are fading to a bruise-like colour. She had a sore throat a month ago. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cellulitis","Nodular vasculitis","Erythema nodosum","Insect bite reactions","Pyoderma gangrenosum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Erythema nodosum is a septal panniculitis producing bilateral tender non-ulcerating nodules 1 to 5 cm on the anterior shins, evolving over 3 to 6 weeks through bruise-like colours. Group A streptococcus is the commonest infectious trigger. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl has moderate papulopustular and mixed acne of the face and upper back that has not responded to eight weeks of a topical antimicrobial. What regimen is recommended?","choices":["An oral antibiotic alone, indefinitely","Oral isotretinoin as monotherapy","A topical retinoid alone","A retinoid, an oral antibiotic, benzoyl peroxide","A topical retinoid and an oral antibiotic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Moderate papulopustular and mixed acne is treated with a topical retinoid and an oral antibiotic and topical benzoyl peroxide. Benzoyl peroxide is specifically added whenever an antibiotic is used, to reduce the risk of bacterial resistance. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has a probable but unconfirmed diagnosis, and a treatment with meaningful adverse effects is being considered. What factors govern the decision to treat when the diagnosis carries some uncertainty?","choices":["Treat whenever disease is possible","Probability, success, tolerance, risk-benefit","Probability of disease alone","Withhold until the diagnosis is certain","The cost of treatment alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The value of treatment versus no treatment is a linear function of the probability of disease, together with the likelihood of success and the ability to tolerate treatment, weighed as risk against benefit. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy has eight light brown macules greater than 5 mm and freckling in both axillae. What condition does this suggest, and what is the freckling called?","choices":["Neurofibromatosis type 1; Crowe's sign","Postinflammatory hyperpigmentation","Multiple lentigines from sun exposure","Neurofibromatosis type 2","Neurofibromatosis type 1; Darier sign"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Café au lait spots are often the first manifestation of neurofibromatosis type 1, defined as light tan to brown macules over 5 mm prepubertally. Intertriginous freckling in the axillary and inguinal regions is Crowe's sign, with freckles smaller than 5 mm. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with a rapidly expanding painful ulcer of the shin is being investigated for an underlying systemic cause. What systemic conditions are most closely associated with pyoderma gangrenosum?","choices":["Sarcoidosis and streptococcal infection","Celiac and autoimmune thyroid disease","Inflammatory bowel disease and arthritis","Venous and arterial insufficiency","Diabetes and gastrointestinal malignancy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pyoderma gangrenosum is a neutrophilic dermatosis with dysregulated innate immune activation, and inflammatory bowel disease heads the list of key associated conditions alongside inflammatory arthritis and haematologic disease. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl develops an abrupt eruption of numerous small raindrop-shaped scaly papules and plaques over the trunk and proximal limbs. Her mother reports she was treated for a sore throat about two and a half weeks ago. She is otherwise well. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Oral methotrexate","Topical mupirocin to the affected areas","Systemic corticosteroids tapered over four weeks","Reassurance, with topical steroids if needed","Oral acitretin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Guttate psoriasis presents as raindrop-shaped erythematous papules and plaques, typically in children 2 to 3 weeks following a streptococcal infection or upper respiratory infection. No treatment is needed, though phototherapy and topical steroids may be used. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 164"},{"q":"A 27-year-old pregnant woman has several lesions on the upper trunk consisting of a central arteriole with radiating capillaries. What is the mechanism, and what should be asked in the history?","choices":["Dilated dermal capillaries present from birth","A vascular tumour after minor trauma","Blood extravasated into the dermis","Capillary proliferation with age; no history needed","Dilated existing vessels; ask about oestrogen and alcohol"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Nevus araneus involves no vascular proliferation and is due to dilation of pre-existing vessels. Oestrogen excess states may be the cause, and lesions resolve after delivery or cessation of hormonal contraception. History should cover pregnancies, hormone use, alcohol, and high-risk medications for liver damage. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has a firm 8 mm nodule on the lower leg with a brown halo and a slightly raised centre. Squeezing the skin on either side makes the lesion dimple downward. It occasionally hurts when knocked. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Melanoma","Hypertrophic scar","Keratoacanthoma","Epidermoid cyst","Dermatofibroma"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Dermatofibroma is a firm 0.5 to 1 cm nodule of dense dermal fibroblasts, most common on the legs, with a hyperpigmented halo and a positive dimple sign. It is the most common painful skin tumour. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man living in a shelter has linear excoriations of the back and waist, while a 6-year-old girl in the same clinic has an itchy scalp. How does the presentation of pediculosis corporis differ from that of pediculosis capitis?","choices":["Linear excoriations of the back and waist","It is always asymptomatic","Scalp involvement with lymphadenopathy","Spread by head-to-head contact","Burrows in the finger webs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Body lice produce pruritus with linear excoriations primarily on the back, neck, shoulders, and waist, and post-inflammatory pigmentation in chronic cases. Head lice affect the scalp with pruritus, low-grade fever, regional lymphadenopathy, and irritability. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has had erythematous scaly patches and plaques on the trunk for four years, several larger than 5 cm, that have been treated as psoriasis and eczema without lasting response. Pruritus is prominent. What features discriminate this from the inflammatory conditions it resembles?","choices":["Scale","Chronicity, treatment resistance, odd sites","Pruritus","A positive potassium hydroxide preparation","Truncal involvement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mycosis fungoides may resemble psoriasis, eczema, or tinea, and the discriminators are chronicity, treatment resistance, and large or oddly distributed lesions. Early morphology is localised or generalised erythematous patches or scaly plaques, usually on the trunk, frequently greater than 5 cm. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with obesity and type 2 diabetes is found on routine examination to have thick, velvety, hyperpigmented plaques on the back of the neck and in both axillae. She has never noticed them. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Postinflammatory hyperpigmentation","Erythrasma","Confluent and reticulated papillomatosis","Acanthosis nigricans","Tinea versicolor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acanthosis nigricans produces velvety hyperpigmented plaques at the neck, axillae, and skin folds, and is strongly associated with insulin resistance, obesity, and type 2 diabetes — all present here. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with a family history of melanoma has more than 100 naevi, several at least 5 mm with irregular, indistinct borders. How is a dysplastic naevus diagnosed, and what is the management?","choices":["Clinical diagnosis; no biopsy needed","Dermoscopy alone","Biopsy, then excise all naevi prophylactically","No follow-up once documented","Biopsy, excising where melanoma is a concern"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Diagnosis of a dysplastic melanocytic nevus is by biopsy. Management includes observation, biopsy of all changing or developing lesions, excision where there is concern for melanoma, and sun protection. Family history is a risk factor, and dysplastic nevus syndrome may produce over 100 naevi by adolescence. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with diabetes has three thickened, discoloured, crumbling toenails with distal onycholysis. He asks for oral terbinafine. What must be done first?","choices":["Start terbinafine empirically","Confirm the fungus first","Obtain a Wood's lamp examination","Start topical antifungal therapy","Remove the nails surgically first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fungus must be confirmed before oral therapy, since many dystrophic nails are not fungal. Testing options include potassium hydroxide microscopy, periodic acid-Schiff stain of nail clippings, fungal culture, or polymerase chain reaction, sampling the most proximal accessible diseased nail bed. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with recurrent tinea pedis is being counselled on preventing further episodes. What patient education point is described as essential?","choices":["Walking barefoot in community showers to allow air drying","Wearing occlusive footwear to protect the skin","Sharing antifungal foot powder with household members","Drying between the toes after bathing","Soaking the feet daily in warm water"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Drying between the toes after bathing is marked as essential, alongside antifungal foot powder for shoes, wearing open-toed sandals when possible, using sandals in community showers, and changing socks frequently. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 3-month-old infant with blistering at sites of minor friction is being investigated to establish where in the skin the split is occurring. What diagnostic test is considered the gold standard for establishing the level of cleavage in epidermolysis bullosa?","choices":["Skin biopsy with transmission electron microscopy","Potassium hydroxide preparation of blister fluid","Immunofluorescence antigen mapping","Genetic testing alone","Direct immunofluorescence for immunoglobulin A deposits"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Skin biopsy with transmission electron microscopy is the gold standard for determining the cleavage plane. Immunofluorescence antigen mapping localises missing or reduced proteins, and genetic testing is confirmatory and guides prognosis and family counselling. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man presents six hours after a day at the beach with painful erythema, warmth, and tenderness across the shoulders and back. There is no blistering. What is the most appropriate acute management?","choices":["Prophylactic oral antibiotics","Cold immersion and delayed anti-inflammatories","Liberal topical anaesthetics","Cool compresses, early anti-inflammatories, hydration","Immediate systemic corticosteroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute management is cool compresses and cool rather than cold water immersion, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs started early to reduce prostaglandin-mediated inflammation and pain, oral hydration, and topical moisturisers. First-degree sunburn resolves in 3 to 5 days with desquamation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has had hidradenitis suppurativa of both axillae for eight years despite antibiotics and repeated drainage, and asks whether anything can cure it. What offers the best chance of permanent cure?","choices":["Wide excision of the affected areas","Incision and drainage of each cyst as it develops","Long-term systemic antibiotics","Intralesional triamcinolone acetonide injections","Topical clindamycin used indefinitely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chances of permanent cure are best with wide excision of the affected areas. Large fluctuant cysts are incised and drained, but that manages an episode rather than curing the disease. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man taking allopurinol for three weeks develops fever, malaise, and painful swallowing, followed by erosions of the lips, mouth, and conjunctivae, and dusky skin lesions with detachment involving about 6 percent of his body surface area. What is the single most important immediate action?","choices":["Admit to a burn unit","Obtain a skin biopsy","Withdraw all suspect medications","Give systemic corticosteroids","Start broad-spectrum antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Immediate causative drug withdrawal is the first step, and earlier withdrawal is strongly associated with improved survival — each day of delay worsens prognosis. Allopurinol is named as the most common trigger worldwide. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man has severe xerosis of both lower legs and a barrier preparation is being chosen for him. What consideration should guide the choice of vehicle?","choices":["Avoid occlusive vehicles in xerosis","Creams and ointments are equally occlusive","Vehicle matters only for corticosteroids","Ointments are more occlusive than creams","Lotions are the most occlusive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Emollients and barrier preparations are the foundation of treatment for many inflammatory conditions, and ointments are generally more occlusive than creams or lotions. Common uses include xerosis, eczema, irritant dermatitis, and diaper dermatitis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has been applying a topical corticosteroid to the same area of eyelid skin for several months. What adverse effects should be anticipated?","choices":["Hyperpigmentation, hypertrichosis, and thickening of the dermis","Nephrotoxicity with prolonged application","Atrophy, striae, telangiectasia, and hypopigmentation","Permanent loss of hair follicles at the treated site","Photosensitivity, dryness, and irritation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prolonged use of topical corticosteroids may cause atrophy, striae, telangiectasia, and hypopigmentation. This is why potency is selected according to site and severity and why the usual course is twice daily for two weeks. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 19-year-old returns from a tropical beach holiday with an intensely itchy, raised, serpentine track on the foot that has advanced a few centimetres over the past two days. What is the diagnosis and treatment?","choices":["Scabies; whole-body permethrin","Cutaneous larva migrans; topical therapy alone","Cutaneous larva migrans; excision or cryotherapy","Cutaneous larva migrans; albendazole or ivermectin","Tungiasis; excision of the flea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cutaneous larva migrans results from larvae of animal nematodes, mostly dog and cat hookworms, in which the human is a dead-end host. The classic lesion is an erythematous, raised, vesicular, linear or serpentine trail progressing 2 to 3 cm per day. Treatment is albendazole 400 mg daily for 3 days or ivermectin. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman is being counselled after a melanoma diagnosis and asks what most determines her outlook. What is the dominant prognostic variable?","choices":["Fitzpatrick skin type","Asymmetry","Anatomic subtype","Lesion diameter","Breslow thickness"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Survival drops sharply with increasing Breslow thickness and with nodal or distant spread. Breslow thickness is the dominant prognostic variable and must be accurately measured at the time of initial biopsy — which is why a shave biopsy that transects the base can compromise staging. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 6-week-old infant has a bright red papule on the shoulder, and the clinician classifies the lesion by the depth of vessel involvement. What are the three types of infantile hemangioma, and which is the most common?","choices":["Congenital, infantile, acquired","Capillary, cavernous, arteriovenous","Superficial, mixed, and deep; deep commonest","Superficial, mixed, and deep; superficial commonest","Proliferative, involuting, involuted"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The superficial type is the most common, with dilated vessels in the dermis surrounded by proliferating endothelial cells, presenting as a bright red papule, plaque, or nodule and previously called a strawberry hemangioma. The deep type is the least common. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"An 84-year-old nursing home resident has thick flaking scale over the hands and trunk but reports no itching at all. What is this presentation, and what is the infection control implication?","choices":["Post-scabietic dermatitis; not infectious","Xerosis of aging; emollients only","Crusted scabies; less infectious than ordinary scabies","Psoriasis; topical corticosteroids","Crusted scabies; highly infectious despite no itch"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Hyperkeratotic or crusted scabies presents as thick flaking scale containing millions of mites. These patients are highly infectious, and pruritus is usually absent — which is exactly why the diagnosis is missed and outbreaks occur. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"An 8-year-old girl has an itchy scalp, low-grade fever, occipital lymphadenopathy, and irritability. Wet combing recovers several moving 2 mm insects, and small oval structures are cemented to hair shafts near the scalp. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea capitis","Atopic dermatitis","Scabies","Seborrheic dermatitis","Pediculosis capitis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Head lice are blood-sucking wingless insects about 2 mm long, common in children aged 3 to 12, producing pruritus, low-grade fever, regional lymphadenopathy, and irritability. Live lice found on combing indicate active infestation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman reports generalised itching every winter with rough, flaking skin worst on the shins. Examination shows dry skin with fine scale and superficial fissuring, without erythematous plaques, vesicles, or burrows. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Stasis dermatitis","Scabies","Xerosis","Atopic dermatitis","Nummular eczema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Xerosis reflects impaired stratum corneum hydration promoted by aging, low humidity, hot water, and detergents. It commonly affects older adults in winter and causes tightness, pruritus, rough scale, and fissuring. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 152"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman is prescribed topical tretinoin for acne. What counselling is most important before she starts?","choices":["Start gradually; expect irritation","Expect greater sun tolerance","Start at full frequency immediately","No pregnancy precautions are needed","Combine with a potent corticosteroid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The important clinical considerations for topical retinoids are irritation, dryness, and photosensitivity; therapy should begin gradually, and pregnancy precautions apply. Common agents include adapalene, tretinoin, tazarotene, and trifarotene. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with numerous naevi asks how she should check her own moles between clinic visits. What do the letters of the ABCDE recognition rule stand for?","choices":["Asymmetry, bleeding, crusting, depth, erosion","Asymmetry, border, colour, dermoscopy, excision","Abscess, bulla, cyst, dermatitis, erythema","Age, burn history, complexion, dysplasia, exposure","Asymmetry, border, colour, diameter, evolution"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Asymmetry means one half does not match the other; border refers to irregular, notched, or poorly defined edges; colour describes variegation with brown, red, white, black, or blue within one lesion; diameter and evolution complete the rule. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man with a family history of melanoma has more than a hundred naevi. Several are at least 6 mm with irregular, indistinct borders and variation in colour within a single lesion. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dysplastic naevus","Common acquired melanocytic naevus","Malignant melanoma","Ephelides","Congenital melanocytic naevus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dysplastic melanocytic naevi are at least 5 mm with irregular indistinct borders, common in Caucasians, with family history as a risk factor. Dysplastic nevus syndrome may produce over 100 naevi by adolescence. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy has several lesions around the nose and mouth that began as small blisters and now show golden-yellow adherent crusts over shallow erosions. There is palpable submandibular lymphadenopathy. He is afebrile. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Impetigo","Herpes simplex virus","Perioral dermatitis","Ecthyma","Contact dermatitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Non-bullous impetigo is the commoner form: a macule evolves into a vesicle or pustule that ruptures, leaving honey-coloured adherent crust over an erosion, typically perioral in children, with regional lymphadenopathy common. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man develops an exaggerated sunburn-like reaction on sun-exposed skin within hours of starting a new medication. The reaction is dose-dependent and occurred on the very first exposure. What mechanism is responsible?","choices":["Phototoxicity, immunologic and dose-independent","Polymorphous light eruption","Phototoxicity, non-immunologic and dose-dependent","Phytophotodermatitis from plant contact","Photoallergy, immunologic and needing sensitisation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Phototoxicity is non-immunologic and dose-dependent — the drug absorbs ultraviolet energy, generating reactive oxygen species and direct cellular damage that resembles an exaggerated sunburn. Photopatch testing is the gold standard for photoallergy, the other mechanism. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman develops a rash on the backs of her hands two days ago that has spread to the forearms and knees. Examination shows lesions with three concentric zones — a dusky centre, a pale ring, and an erythematous rim — fixed in place for four days. She had a cold sore two weeks ago. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Erythema multiforme","Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Fixed drug eruption","Pityriasis rosea","Urticaria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Erythema multiforme produces characteristic fixed target lesions with three concentric zones in an acral distribution. Over 50 percent of cases are triggered by herpes simplex virus, which the recent cold sore supplies. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man has painful erythema and warmth of the shoulders after a day outdoors, with no blistering and changes confined to the epidermis. How is this classified, and what course should be expected?","choices":["First degree; 1 to 2 weeks with infection risk","First degree; 3 to 5 days with desquamation","Third degree; surgical referral","Second degree; 3 to 5 days","Second degree; prophylactic antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. First-degree sunburn produces erythema, warmth, and tenderness confined to the epidermis without blistering, and resolves in 3 to 5 days with desquamation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has folliculitis that has recurred repeatedly despite topical treatment, and cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus each time. What additional measure addresses the likely reservoir?","choices":["An antibacterial soap alone","A longer course of the same cream","Mupirocin to the nares for the carrier state","Oral ciprofloxacin","A potassium hydroxide mount of a hair"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent or recalcitrant folliculitis raises the Staphylococcus aureus carrier state, and mupirocin ointment is used for it. Nasal swabs of the patient and family members are taken to evaluate carriage. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has multiple light to dark brown macules with well-defined but irregular borders that tend to coalesce over his shoulders, where he has had severe sunburns. What is the underlying process and its epidemiological association?","choices":["Basal melanocyte proliferation with more melanin","T cell destruction of melanocytes","Somatic mutation present from birth","Hyperinsulinaemia driving keratinocyte growth","Dermal capillary proliferation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Solar lentigines arise from proliferation of basal melanocytes with increased melanin production and are strongly associated with older age, present in about 90 percent by age 50, along with sun damage, tanning, a history of ephelides, and birth control use. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has widespread intensely itchy raised weals that have come and gone for four days. Individual lesions last a few hours, blanch fully, and leave no mark. She has no fever, joint pain, or bruising at resolved sites. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Erythema multiforme","Contact dermatitis","Mastocytosis","Urticarial vasculitis","Urticaria"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Urticaria results from mast cell degranulation producing transient pruritic weals. Individual lesions last less than 24 hours, blanch fully, and resolve without residual change. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with obesity has candidal intertrigo of both inframammary folds. Beyond antifungal therapy, what environmental measure is central to treatment?","choices":["Increase the frequency of hot showers","Dry the folds and reduce friction and occlusion","Apply a high-potency corticosteroid","Bind the folds tightly together","Apply an occlusive ointment to the folds"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Correcting the environment is central: gently dry the folds, reduce friction and occlusion, use moisture-wicking fabric or absorbent material, and address incontinence or hyperhidrosis. Intertrigo itself is caused by friction, moisture, and heat trapped in body folds. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman with several common warts on the hands asks whether treatment will get rid of the virus for good. What treatment principle applies to all cutaneous warts?","choices":["Biopsy every wart first","Treat all warts at diagnosis","Excise deeply; they reach the dermis","No therapy eradicates the virus; recurrence happens","Cryotherapy eradicates it and prevents recurrence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. No therapy eradicates human papillomavirus with certainty and recurrence can occur. Treatment is chosen according to location, symptoms, age, pregnancy status, immune status, and the risk of scarring or dyspigmentation, avoiding excessive freezing or destruction. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 149"},{"q":"A 4-year-old girl has a typical history and examination for atopic dermatitis. Her parents ask whether blood tests are needed to confirm the diagnosis. What is the most accurate response?","choices":["Patch test every child at diagnosis","Check an immunoglobulin E level in every child","Culture every child at diagnosis","It is clinical; no routine laboratory testing","Biopsy every child before treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is usually clinical, supported by family history, personal history of atopy, and a recurrent history of rash. Immunoglobulin E may be elevated but is not routinely tested, and routine laboratory testing is not required. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 6-year-old unvaccinated child has a generalised pruritic eruption concentrated on the trunk, scalp, and face. Macules, papules, vesicles, and crusts are all present at the same time. What feature is most characteristic?","choices":["Lesions confined to a single dermatome","Lesions with honey-coloured crusting around the nose and mouth","Lesions limited to the palms and soles","Lesions in multiple stages of healing appearing simultaneously","Lesions all at the same stage of evolution"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Primary varicella-zoster virus infection causes a generalised pruritic eruption in multiple stages of healing — macules progress to papules, vesicles, and crusts, with several stages appearing simultaneously. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman is found on routine examination to have several smooth, firm, deep red papules under 5 mm on the trunk that blanch with pressure. She has never noticed them. What education should be given?","choices":["Benign capillary proliferation","They indicate liver disease","Biopsy is needed to diagnose them","Benign, but can progress to malignancy","Caused by increased melanocytes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cherry angiomas are acquired vascular lesions formed by capillary or venule proliferation, very common and increasing with age. Treatment is unnecessary unless they bother the patient, and patients should be aware that new lesions will likely develop and there is no way to prevent them. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man presents with a single intensely itchy patch on the posterior neck that has been present for over a year. He describes rubbing it frequently, particularly when stressed or trying to fall asleep. Examination shows a well-demarcated thickened plaque with exaggerated skin markings, excoriation, scale, and altered pigmentation. What is the central principle of management?","choices":["An oral antifungal","Break the itch-scratch cycle","Indefinite potent topical corticosteroid","Surgical excision of the plaque","Reassurance alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lichen simplex chronicus results from repeated rubbing and scratching, so management centres on breaking the itch-scratch cycle with education, treatment of the trigger, emollients, behavioural substitution, nail care, and safe physical occlusion, alongside a limited course of an appropriate potent topical corticosteroid. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 183"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy has culture-confirmed tinea capitis and the species result will determine which drug he receives. Which oral agent is generally favoured for Trichophyton infection, and which for Microsporum?","choices":["Griseofulvin for Trichophyton and terbinafine for Microsporum","Terbinafine for both organisms","Terbinafine for Trichophyton and griseofulvin for Microsporum","Fluconazole for both organisms","Topical ketoconazole for both organisms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Terbinafine is generally favoured for Trichophyton infection and griseofulvin is often favoured for Microsporum infection, which is why identifying the organism by culture matters before prolonged systemic therapy. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl is evaluated for a lesion on the finger pad that appeared rapidly after she injured the finger and bleeds easily when bumped. Examination shows a bright red, friable, pedunculated papule. What is the diagnosis, and what is notable about its name?","choices":["Cherry angioma","Pyogenic granuloma, a granulomatous infection","Pyogenic granuloma; the name is a misnomer","Dermatofibroma","Amelanotic melanoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pyogenic granuloma is an acquired vascular lesion that is misnamed, being neither infectious nor granulomatous. It is a benign vascular tumour common in children, young adults, and pregnancy, arising as a response to injury or hormonal factors, most often on the head, neck, and fingers. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with cellulitis of the leg is warned that the area may look worse on the first day of antibiotics. What explains this, and when should therapy be reconsidered?","choices":["Bacterial killing inflames it; review after 7 days","Bacterial killing inflames it; review at 48 hours","The antibiotic is failing; change it on day one","Devitalised tissue needing debridement","The patient has become allergic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cellulitis may look and feel worse during the first day because sudden destruction of pathogens releases potent enzymes that increase local inflammation. Fever usually resolves within 24 hours, and persistence beyond 48 hours should prompt a change in antimicrobial therapy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy has scaly patches on the scalp with areas of hair loss. Examination shows grey ring-formed patches with perifollicular papules and visible black dots where hairs have fractured at the surface. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Psoriasis","Tinea capitis","Trichotillomania","Alopecia areata","Seborrheic dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tinea capitis is the most common fungal infection in children, producing red papules progressing to greyish ring-formed patches with perifollicular papules, scaly patches, and alopecia. Black dot tinea capitis reflects fracture of the hair. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A clinician searches for published evidence to answer a specific question about a 65-year-old smoker with chest pain and haemoptysis, and finds none that applies. What are the limitations of applying evidence-based medicine at the bedside?","choices":["It applies only to surgical decisions","It removes the need for judgement","It is time consuming and evidence is often unavailable","It always answers definitively but is expensive","It cannot be combined with guidelines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The limitations given are that the process is time consuming and that many clinical questions do not have relevant evidence to answer them. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with obesity has a well-demarcated erythematous patch in the inframammary fold with several small papules and pustules on adjacent skin just beyond the main area of redness. What finding identifies the organism, and what topical choice covers both possibilities?","choices":["Coral-red fluorescence; topical erythromycin","Satellite lesions; a topical azole covers both","Honey-coloured crusting; topical mupirocin","Central clearing; terbinafine covers both","Satellite lesions; nystatin covers both"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Satellite papules and pustules accompany the well-demarcated erythematous patches of candidal intertrigo. Topical nystatin treats Candida only, whereas topical azoles treat both Candida and dermatophytes. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy has several light brown macules on the trunk, and his 30-year-old father has similar but larger ones. What size threshold defines a café au lait macule as significant before and after puberty?","choices":["Size is not part of the definition","Over 5 mm before puberty, over 15 mm after","Over 1 cm at any age","Over 5 mm at any age","Over 15 mm before puberty, over 5 mm after"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Café au lait spots are light tan to brown macules greater than 5 mm prepubertally and greater than 15 mm postpubertally. They are often the first manifestation of neurofibromatosis type 1 and are usually present at birth or appear during the first year. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 44-year-old fair-skinned woman newly diagnosed with rosacea asks which trigger is most important to control. What is the most accurate answer?","choices":["Alcohol, described as the most universal trigger","Topical corticosteroid use, which is the primary cause","Cold weather, which is the dominant environmental trigger","Sun exposure, described as the most universal trigger","Dairy products, which drive Demodex overgrowth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sun exposure is named as the most universal trigger of rosacea, alongside heat, alcohol, spicy foods, and stress. Chronic ultraviolet exposure is also a listed risk factor. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A newborn boy has a large flat brown plaque with a slightly uneven border and a pebbly surface over the posterior scalp and upper back, present at delivery. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nevus flammeus","Congenital melanocytic naevus","Cafe au lait macule","Nevus spilus","Dermal melanocytosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Congenital melanocytic naevi arise from somatic mutations and are evident at birth or shortly after, presenting as flat brown patches or plaques with smooth or slightly uneven borders and sometimes a pebbly, rugose, or verrucous surface. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy develops multiple erythematous papules arranged in linear streaks after handling a caterpillar. What is the term for the aggregate of medical effects caused by caterpillars, moths, and butterflies, and what is a specific step in management?","choices":["Arachnidism; antivenom","Lepidopterism; strip the hairs off","Erucism; systemic corticosteroids only","Tungiasis; excision","Cercarial dermatitis; oatmeal baths"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lepidopterism is the aggregate of medical effects caused by caterpillars, moths, and butterflies. Gypsy moth caterpillar contact causes erucism, a pruritic dermatitis with erythematous papules in linear streaks. Management is symptomatic, and the hairs are removed by stripping. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman has a firm, bulbous nodule at the site of an ear piercing performed two years ago. The lesion extends well beyond the original piercing site and has continued to enlarge. What distinguishes this from a hypertrophic scar?","choices":["It developed within four weeks","It is always painless","It stays within the wound margins","It extends beyond the wound margins","It flattens with time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A keloid extends beyond the wound site margins, develops slowly, continues to enlarge for months to years, and shows no regression with a tendency to recur. A hypertrophic scar develops rapidly within four weeks, remains within the wound margins, and flattens with time. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A furuncle on the neck of a 37-year-old man is incised and drained, and the culture grows methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. Which oral regimens are appropriate?","choices":["Topical mupirocin alone","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, doxycycline, or clindamycin","Ciprofloxacin or acetic acid soaks","Penicillin V or amoxicillin","Dicloxacillin or cephalexin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is cultured or suspected, the oral options given are trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, doxycycline, and clindamycin. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has a basal cell carcinoma on the nasal ala that has recurred after a previous excision. When should Mohs micrographic surgery be considered for basal cell carcinoma?","choices":["Only after topical therapy fails twice","High-risk sites, recurrence, or tissue sparing","Every basal cell carcinoma","Only for tumours over 5 cm","Never; surgery is not required"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mohs surgery is indicated for high-risk anatomic sites, recurrent tumours, aggressive histology, or where tissue-sparing is needed. The overall goal is complete eradication with minimal cosmetic and functional deformity. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 36-year-old pregnant woman and her 7-year-old son both develop erythema migrans after a camping trip. What is first-line oral antibiotic therapy for Lyme disease, and what alternative applies to children and pregnant women with early disease?","choices":["Doxycycline, or amoxicillin in pregnancy","Topical permethrin to the bite","Intravenous ceftriaxone for all stages","Amoxicillin, with doxycycline for failure","No antibiotics for stage 1"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics are indicated in all stages. Doxycycline is first line, with amoxicillin as an alternative first-line agent for early disease including erythema migrans, particularly in children and pregnant women. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has greasy yellow scale over the scalp, eyebrows, and nasolabial folds. What organism is responsible for seborrheic dermatitis, and where is the condition typically found?","choices":["Autoimmunity against sebaceous glands","Malassezia overgrowth at flexural sites","Malassezia overgrowth at sebum-rich sites","An acquired dermatophyte infection","Staphylococcus aureus colonisation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Seborrheic dermatitis is caused by overgrowth of Malassezia, which is normal skin flora. It is typically found on the scalp as dandruff, and on the eyebrows, under facial hair, in the nasolabial creases, forehead, nose, behind the ears, and in the external auditory canal. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Four women in their twenties present on the same clinic list with acne of differing severity and history. For which patient is a combined oral contraceptive most appropriate as acne therapy?","choices":["Any adolescent, as first-line therapy","Hyperandrogenism unresponsive to topicals","Mild comedonal acne, never treated","A woman planning pregnancy soon","A man with papulopustular acne"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oral contraceptives are considered for patients with hyperandrogenism and when acne is unresponsive to both topical retinoids and topical or oral antibiotics. Oestrogen decreases sebum production and reduces ovarian androgen production. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has a painful eruption on the vermilion border preceded by a day of tingling and burning. Examination shows grouped vesicles on an erythematous base, some broken down into shallow painful ulcers. She has had similar episodes before. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Aphthous ulceration","Herpes simplex virus","Angular cheilitis","Impetigo","Herpes zoster"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Herpes simplex produces grouped vesicles on an erythematous base that break down into shallow painful ulcers, preceded by tenderness, paraesthesia, or burning, and it is characterised by latency with lifelong recurrence at the same site. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with obesity has a burning rash under both breasts. Examination shows well-demarcated beefy erythematous patches in the folds with peripheral scale and several small papules and pustules on adjacent skin just beyond the main area of redness. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cutaneous candidiasis","Inverse psoriasis","Tinea corporis","Contact dermatitis","Erythrasma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Candidal intertrigo produces well-demarcated erythematous patches in warm moist folds with satellite papules and pustules beyond the main area, and pruritus with burning pain is typical. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 71"}]};
